{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Fuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayue","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiayue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tahseen","familyName":"Rabbani","name":"Rabbani, Tahseen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Sohaib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divyakant","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Divyakant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amr El","familyName":"Abbadi","name":"Abbadi, Amr El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paritosh","familyName":"Aggarwal","name":"Aggarwal, Paritosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anupam","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Anupam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitris","familyName":"Tsirogiannis","name":"Tsirogiannis, Dimitris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Access Paths for Efficient Ordering with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T01:44:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:11:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T06:35:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:26:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T23:05:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:17:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-14T05:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:35:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-09T15:39:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-12T01:11:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we present the \\texttt{LLM ORDER BY} semantic operator as a logical abstraction and conduct a systematic study of its physical implementations. First, we propose several improvements to existing semantic sorting algorithms and introduce a semantic-aware external merge sort algorithm. Our extensive evaluation reveals that no single implementation offers universal optimality on all datasets. From our evaluations, we observe a general scaling relationship between sorting cost and the ordering quality for comparison-based algorithms. Building on these insights, we design a budget-aware optimizer that utilizes heuristic rules, LLM-as-Judge evaluation, and consensus aggregation to dynamically select the near-optimal access path for LLM ORDER BY. In our extensive evaluations, our optimizer consistently achieves ranking accuracy on par with or superior to the best static methods across all benchmarks. We believe that this work provides foundational insights into the principled optimization of semantic operators essential for building robust, large-scale LLM-powered analytic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:29:25Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:29:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:48:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo Gioele","familyName":"Collu","name":"Collu, Matteo Gioele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Salviati","name":"Salviati, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Confalonieri","name":"Confalonieri, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Conti","name":"Conti, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Apruzzese","name":"Apruzzese, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Misleading Large Language Models used (or misused) in Scientific Peer-Reviewing via Hidden Prompt-Injection Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T14:57:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:43:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T09:37:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:25:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T13:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:44:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3803804","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being integrated into the scientific peer-review process, raising new questions about their reliability and resilience to manipulation. In this work, we investigate the potential for hidden prompt injection attacks, where authors embed adversarial text within a paper's PDF to influence the LLM-generated review. We begin by formalising three distinct threat models that envision attackers with different motivations -- not all of which implying malicious intent. For each threat model, we design adversarial prompts that remain invisible to human readers yet can steer an LLM's output toward the author's desired outcome. Using a user study with domain scholars, we derive four representative reviewing prompts used to elicit peer reviews from LLMs. We then evaluate the robustness of our adversarial prompts across (i) different reviewing prompts, (ii) different commercial LLM-based systems, and (iii) different peer-reviewed papers. Our results show that adversarial prompts can reliably mislead the LLM, sometimes in ways that adversely affect a \"honest-but-lazy\" reviewer. Finally, we propose and empirically assess methods to reduce detectability of adversarial prompts under automated content checks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM TAISAP"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-08-29T01:43:02Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:43:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:01:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04466","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04466","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04466","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Declan","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Declan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephanie C. Y.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Stephanie C. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew Kyle","familyName":"Lampinen","name":"Lampinen, Andrew Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Just-in-time and distributed task representations in language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T23:08:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T01:34:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T23:48:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:12:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many of language models' impressive capabilities originate from their in-context learning: based on instructions or examples, they can infer and perform new tasks without weight updates. In this work, we investigate when representations for new tasks are formed in language models, and how these representations change over the course of context. We study two different task representations: those that are ''transferrable'' -- vector representations that can transfer task contexts to another model instance, even without the full prompt -- and simpler representations of high-level task categories. We show that transferrable task representations evolve in non-monotonic and sporadic ways, while task identity representations persist throughout the context. Specifically, transferrable task representations exhibit a two-fold locality. They successfully condense evidence when more examples are provided in the context. But this evidence accrual process exhibits strong temporal locality along the sequence dimension, coming online only at certain tokens -- despite task identity being reliably decodable throughout the context. In some cases, transferrable task representations also show semantic locality, capturing a small task ''scope'' such as an independent subtask. Language models thus represent new tasks on the fly through both an inert, sustained sensitivity to the task and an active, just-in-time representation to support inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04466","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:25:38Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaan","familyName":"Nagy","name":"Nagy, Shaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadia","familyName":"Polikarpova","name":"Polikarpova, Nadia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loris","familyName":"D'Antoni","name":"D'Antoni, Loris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChopChop: a Programmable Framework for Semantically Constraining the Output of Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.3.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T04:51:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:14:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:17:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models (LMs) can generate code but cannot guarantee its correctness$\\unicode{x2014}$often producing outputs that violate type safety, program invariants, or other semantic properties. Constrained decoding offers a solution by restricting generation to only produce programs that satisfy user-defined properties. However, existing methods are either limited to syntactic constraints or rely on brittle, ad hoc encodings of semantic properties over token sequences rather than program structure.\n We present ChopChop, the first programmable framework for constraining the output of LMs with respect to semantic properties. ChopChop introduces a principled way to construct constrained decoders based on analyzing the space of programs a prefix represents. It formulates this analysis as a realizability problem which is solved via coinduction, connecting token-level generation with structural reasoning over programs. We demonstrate ChopChop's generality by using it to enforce (1) equivalence to a reference program and (2) type safety. Across a range of models and tasks, ChopChop improves success rates while maintaining practical decoding latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:30:50Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:30:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guofu","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Guofu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taotao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiqun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriLoRA: Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with Verifiable Security via Zero-Knowledge Proofs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T08:14:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:20:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-09T15:20:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:50:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T04:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) is crucial for adapting them to specific tasks, yet it remains computationally demanding and raises concerns about correctness and privacy, particularly in untrusted environments. Although parameter-efficient methods like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) significantly reduce resource requirements, ensuring the security and verifiability of fine-tuning under zero-knowledge constraints remains an unresolved challenge. To address this, we introduce VeriLoRA, the first framework to integrate LoRA fine-tuning with zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs), achieving provable security and correctness. VeriLoRA employs advanced cryptographic techniques -- such as lookup arguments, sumcheck protocols, and polynomial commitments -- to verify both arithmetic and non-arithmetic operations in Transformer-based architectures. The framework provides end-to-end verifiability for forward propagation, backward propagation, and parameter updates during LoRA fine-tuning, while safeguarding the privacy of model parameters and training data. Leveraging GPU-based implementations, VeriLoRA demonstrates practicality and efficiency through experimental validation on open-source LLMs like LLaMA, scaling up to 13 billion parameters. By combining parameter-efficient fine-tuning with ZKPs, VeriLoRA bridges a critical gap, enabling secure and trustworthy deployment of LLMs in sensitive or untrusted environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted for publication at the Network and Distributed System Security Symposium (NDSS) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:26:01Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:26:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuguang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel Z.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lionel Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyuan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RepoMark: A Data-Usage Auditing Framework for Code Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T09:01:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:23:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T06:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:28:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T02:58:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:12:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of Large Language Models (LLMs) for code generation has transformed software development by automating coding tasks with unprecedented efficiency.\n However, the training of these models on open-source code repositories (e.g., from GitHub) raises critical ethical and legal concerns, particularly regarding data authorization and open-source license compliance. Developers are increasingly questioning whether model trainers have obtained proper authorization before using repositories for training, especially given the lack of transparency in data collection.\n To address these concerns, we propose a novel data marking framework RepoMark to audit the data usage of code LLMs. Our method enables auditors to verify whether their code has been used in training, while ensuring semantic preservation, imperceptibility, and theoretical false detection rate (FDR) guarantees. By generating multiple semantically equivalent code variants, RepoMark introduces data marks into the code files, and during detection, RepoMark leverages a novel ranking-based hypothesis test to detect model behavior difference on trained data. Compared to prior data auditing approaches, RepoMark significantly enhances data efficiency, allowing effective auditing even when the user's repository possesses only a small number of code files.\n Experiments demonstrate that RepoMark achieves a detection success rate over 90\\% on small code repositories under a strict FDR guarantee of 5\\%. This represents a significant advancement over existing data marking techniques, all of which only achieve accuracy below 55\\% under identical settings. This further validates RepoMark as a robust, theoretically sound, and promising solution for enhancing transparency in code LLM training, which can safeguard the rights of code authors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:26:57Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:26:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:55:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhonghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weilin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Weilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengying","familyName":"Huan","name":"Huan, Chengying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Inference by Hiding Offloading Latency with Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T15:25:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:40:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T09:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:34:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in Mixture of Experts (MoE) models have significantly increased their parameter scale as well as model performance. Extensive offloading techniques have been proposed to address the GPU memory limitations of MoE inference. However, due to the I/O bottleneck and sparse computation of MoE models, existing offloading techniques still suffer from low hardware utilization. To fully utilize the hardware resources, we propose SpecMoEOff, which employs the speculative decoding technique to enlarge the workload of each expert. SpecMoEOff orchestrates the GPU and CPU by both theoretical and empirical roofline analysis. In addition, we develop a dedicated CPU chunked attention verification kernel to fit the speculative decoding in offloading scenarios as well as minimizing the additional overhead led by draft models. SpecMoEOff further integrates an optimizer to automatically tune the hyperparameters of speculative decoding under given hardware and workload. Experimental results show that SpecMoEOff achieves up to 2.5x decode throughput improvement over the state-of-the-art MoE offloading techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:34:00Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:34:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:00:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farnoush Rezaei","familyName":"Jafari","name":"Jafari, Farnoush Rezaei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Eberle","name":"Eberle, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashkan","familyName":"Khakzar","name":"Khakzar, Ashkan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neel","familyName":"Nanda","name":"Nanda, Neel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RelP: Faithful and Efficient Circuit Discovery in Language Models via Relevance Patching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T23:09:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:09:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T16:01:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:55:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Activation patching is a standard method in mechanistic interpretability for localizing the components of a model responsible for specific behaviors, but it is computationally expensive to apply at scale. Attribution patching offers a faster, gradient-based approximation, yet suffers from noise and reduced reliability in deep, highly non-linear networks. In this work, we introduce Relevance Patching (RelP), which replaces the local gradients in attribution patching with propagation coefficients derived from Layer-wise Relevance Propagation (LRP). LRP propagates the network's output backward through the layers, redistributing relevance to lower-level components according to local propagation rules that ensure properties such as relevance conservation or improved signal-to-noise ratio. Like attribution patching, RelP requires only two forward passes and one backward pass, maintaining computational efficiency while improving faithfulness. We validate RelP across a range of models and tasks, showing that it more accurately approximates activation patching than standard attribution patching, particularly when analyzing residual stream and MLP outputs in the Indirect Object Identification (IOI) task. For instance, for MLP outputs in GPT-2 Large, attribution patching achieves a Pearson correlation of 0.006, whereas RelP reaches 0.956, highlighting the improvement offered by RelP. Additionally, we compare the faithfulness of sparse feature circuits identified by RelP and Integrated Gradients (IG), showing that RelP achieves comparable faithfulness without the extra computational cost associated with IG."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:22:48Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:22:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:09:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muskan","familyName":"Saraf","name":"Saraf, Muskan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad Rezvani","familyName":"Boroujeni","name":"Boroujeni, Sajjad Rezvani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Beaudry","name":"Beaudry, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Abedi","name":"Abedi, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Bush","name":"Bush, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Label-Induced Bias in Large Language Model Self- and Cross-Evaluations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T18:59:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-02T16:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-03T02:17:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T20:01:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:08:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as evaluators of text quality, yet the validity of their judgments remains underexplored. This study investigates systematic bias in self- and cross-model evaluations across three prominent LLMs: ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude. We designed a controlled experiment in which blog posts authored by each model were evaluated by all three models under four labeling conditions: no attribution, true attribution, and two false-attribution scenarios. Evaluations employed both holistic preference voting and granular quality ratings across three dimensions Coherence, Informativeness, and Conciseness with all scores normalized to percentages for direct comparison. Our findings reveal pronounced asymmetries in model judgments: the \"Claude\" label consistently elevated scores regardless of actual authorship, while the \"Gemini\" label systematically depressed them. False attribution frequently reversed preference rankings, producing shifts of up to 50 percentage points in voting outcomes and up to 12 percentage points in quality ratings. Notably, Gemini exhibited severe self-deprecation under true labels, while Claude demonstrated intensified self-preference. These results demonstrate that perceived model identity can substantially distort both high-level judgments and fine-grained quality assessments, independent of content quality. Our findings challenge the reliability of LLM-as-judge paradigms and underscore the critical need for blind evaluation protocols and diverse multi-model validation frameworks to ensure fairness and validity in automated text evaluation and LLM benchmarking."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:20:33Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:20:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:15:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiki","familyName":"Takenami","name":"Takenami, Yoshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin Jou","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yin Jou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yugo","familyName":"Murawaki","name":"Murawaki, Yugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Does Cognitive Bias Affect Large Language Models? A Case Study on the Anchoring Effect in Price Negotiation Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T18:13:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:02:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T02:08:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:16:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cognitive biases, well-studied in humans, can also be observed in LLMs, affecting their reliability in real-world applications. This paper investigates the anchoring effect in LLM-driven price negotiations. To this end, we instructed seller LLM agents to apply the anchoring effect and evaluated negotiations using not only an objective metric but also a subjective metric. Experimental results show that LLMs are influenced by the anchoring effect like humans. Additionally, we investigated the relationship between the anchoring effect and factors such as reasoning and personality. It was shown that reasoning models are less prone to the anchoring effect, suggesting that the long chain of thought mitigates the effect. However, we found no significant correlation between personality traits and susceptibility to the anchoring effect. These findings contribute to a deeper understanding of cognitive biases in LLMs and to the realization of safe and responsible application of LLMs in society."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures. Accepted to EMNLP 2025 findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:19:54Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:19:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqin","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Haoqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi Heem","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Chi Heem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cihang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Cihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Percy","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Percy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AHELM: A Holistic Evaluation of Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T07:40:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-02T17:58:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-04T00:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluations of audio-language models (ALMs) -- multimodal models that take interleaved audio and text as input and output text -- are hindered by the lack of standardized benchmarks; most benchmarks measure only one or two capabilities and omit evaluative aspects such as fairness or safety. Furthermore, comparison across models is difficult as separate evaluations test a limited number of models and use different prompting methods and inference parameters. To address these shortfalls, we introduce AHELM, a benchmark that aggregates various datasets -- including 2 new synthetic audio-text datasets called PARADE, which evaluates the ALMs on avoiding stereotypes, and CoRe-Bench, which measures reasoning over conversational audio through inferential multi-turn question answering -- to holistically measure the performance of ALMs across 10 aspects we have identified as important to the development and usage of ALMs: audio perception, knowledge, reasoning, emotion detection, bias, fairness, multilinguality, robustness, toxicity, and safety. We also standardize the prompts, inference parameters, and evaluation metrics to ensure equitable comparisons across models. We test 14 open-weight and closed-API ALMs from 3 developers and 3 additional simple baseline systems each consisting of an automatic speech recognizer and a language model. Our results show that while Gemini 2.5 Pro ranks top in 5 out of 10 aspects, it exhibits group unfairness ($p=0.01$) on ASR tasks whereas most of the other models do not. We also find that the baseline systems perform reasonably well on AHELM, with one ranking 6th overall despite having only speech-to-text capabilities. For transparency, all raw prompts, model generations, and outputs are available on our website at https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/audio/v1.0.0. AHELM is intended to be a living benchmark and new datasets and models will be added over time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:25:36Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:25:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-04T02:05:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":11,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00303%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20863%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21393%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00360%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04466%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21432%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21706%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21258%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21164%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21137%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21376)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=20"}}